  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-3)(x-1)^{2}?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
  "worked_solution": "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-3)(0-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238854/assets/baff75d3cb8119a39819b82cca053f686ce33ebfc86f963422e1a0653a9f842b.png)",
